r name to determine if it's closed. Finally, I'll return the result as-is without processing. I need to output the status. I'll write code to perform these steps. I'll not use any knowledge base functions as they are not relevant to this task (navigate_to_activity_dashboard and navigate_to_project_details are for navigation to other pages, not for sorting or opening issues).\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Sort issues by 'Updated date' to get the latest updated\n    sort_button = page.get_by_role('button', name='Created date')\n    await sort_button.click()\n    # Wait for dropdown to appear\n    await page.wait_for_timeout(1000)\n    # Select 'Updated date' from the dropdown\n    updated_date_option = page.get_by_role('option', name='Updated date')\n    await updated_date_option.click()\n    # Wait for the page to update after sorting\n    await page.wait_for_load_state('networkidle')\n    # Find issues with 'feature' in the title (case-insensitive)\n    # Use get_by_role('link') and filter by name containing 'feature'\n    feature_issues = page.get_by_role('link').filter(has_text='feature')\n    # Get the first issue (latest after sorting by updated date)\n    first_feature_issue = feature_issues.first\n    # Click to open the issue\n    await first_feature_issue.click()\n    # Wait for the issue page to load\n    await page.wait_for_load_state('networkidle')\n    # Check if the issue is closed by looking for status indicators\n    # Common roles: button with name 'Close issue' or 'Reopen issue', or status text\n    # Try to find a button that indicates closed state\n    closed_button = page.get_by_role('button', name='Reopen issue')\n    open_button = page.get_by_role('button', name='Close issue')\n    # Determine status based on which button is present\n    if await closed_button.count() > 0:\n        status = 'closed'\n    elif await open_button.count() > 0:\n        status = 'open'\n    else:\n        # Fallback: look for status text or ot